  "data": {
    "A": "Explore exact frequency-count alternatives for the per-band prefix scan, especially a bounded direct-address table with generation stamps or a touched-entry reset. The constraints bound stored prefix sums to [-999000, 999000]; treat queries outside the stored-key range as absent, and retain 64-bit answer accumulation. Measure memory and cache costs against the current fixed hash table rather than assuming direct addressing wins. The measured flat-table candidate at 0.0846 s is slower than the best observed candidate, so do not simply reproduce it.",
    "B": "Refine the best measured serial kernel: orient storage so bands run along the shorter dimension, incrementally update contiguous compressed sums, and use a generation-stamped open-addressed frequency table. Investigate low-risk table sizing/hash and loop improvements, measuring end-to-end time and checking correctness for all valid shapes and values. Keep the total answer 64-bit. The int-key variant measured 0.05545 s versus 0.05530 s for the 64-bit-prefix variant, so key narrowing is not an established speedup.",
    "C": "Test whether parallelism helps when applied to the optimized short-axis band kernel, not just the original map-based loop. Use private per-worker compressed sums and frequency tables, 64-bit subtotals, and balanced band-pair ranges; tune a workload-based serial threshold and cap workers at the target's four cores. Existing OpenMP/map candidates measured 0.0998 s and 0.1053 s, both slower than the best observed serial result, so treat parallelization as a hypothesis and compare full-run measurements against that serial baseline.",
    "G": "Use the 0.05530 s B candidate as the measured starting point; do not integrate slower candidates wholesale. Evaluate promising A lookup or C parallel changes one at a time, then measure the actual combined program and rerun correctness checks. Gains may interact or regress, so retain a combination only when its measured result justifies it. Preserve general correctness, including negative entries and 64-bit counts, and do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.",
    "analysis": "This is Problem A: the baseline is a column-band prefix-sum count with O(MN²) work. The strongest measured programs use the exact shorter-dimension band formulation and fixed open addressing: 0.05530 s and 0.05545 s, against the correct baseline at 0.25254 s. The flat-table candidate measured 0.08457 s; the two map-based OpenMP candidates measured 0.09978 s and 0.10534 s; orientation alone measured 0.24978 s. The target is ARM64 with four CPU cores and no GPU. For valid dimensions below 1000 and entries bounded by 1000 in magnitude, a band prefix has magnitude at most 999000, while the total number of submatrices can exceed 32-bit range; use those bounds only with explicit correctness reasoning. Measurements guide experiments but do not establish that independently measured changes will compose."
